  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on computing mean differences from a reference value (here, zero), which reduces to expected value. The individual did not explicitly calculate expected values or differences from a reference point, nor did they mention such a concept. However, they focused on the higher outcome of B and treated the probabilities as nearly equal, effectively giving more weight to the higher payout—this aligns qualitatively with the reason’s emphasis on favorable mean differences, especially since B has a higher expected value. While the cognitive process is not identical, the justification (prioritizing higher outcome when probabilities are similar) leads to the same preference and reflects a similar weighting of outcome magnitude over small probability differences. The absence of explicit reference to differences from a reference value or structured averaging reduces confidence that the formal reason was used, but the gist of favoring B due to higher payout under nearly equal chances is consistent. Thus, the decision reason may have been implicitly reflected, but not clearly or fully applied.",
  "final_assessment_score": 60
